values
  ('2ddfb6cf-243b-59c9-9b95-7cbc1812d9e7', 'the-with-adjectives-for-groups', 'The + adjective for a group: the rich, the French', 'english_grammar', 'language_rule', 'Use the + an adjective to mean a whole group of people: the rich, the elderly, the unemployed. Use the + a nationality ending in -ch, -sh, -ese or -ss to mean the people of that country: the French, the Swiss, the Chinese.', 'The rich and the French: the adjective or nationality means a whole group.', 'The + adjective = a crowd of people.', 'built-in', 'public', true),
  ('bf492ef0-a5d1-5c76-b3ab-2ddbe93418bc', 'a-after-such-what-quite', 'Such a, what a, quite a: the article comes after the word', 'english_grammar', 'language_rule', 'After such, what, quite, rather and half, a singular countable noun takes a or an after that word: such a day, what a view, half an hour. With plural or uncountable nouns there is no article: such nice people, what beautiful weather.', 'Say: such a long day, what a view, half an hour.', 'Such, what, quite: the article comes after, one thing only.', 'built-in', 'public', true)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

